// generated by: flint, validated by: [mpmath, decimal] ; near--1 pole: t = -1 + 10^-m drives the argument 1+t to 10^-m, m decimal orders below 1 - maximal cancellation in forming 1+t, and the region where this oracle's own mpmath adapter under-budgeted working precision until issue #66 (it sized against the result magnitude ~2.3m and ignored the derivative 1/(1+t), which destroys m digits). Result is exactly -m*ln(10). m sweeps every golden scale so each tier meets the pole at its own full depth, m = S being the last representable point before the t <= -1 domain panic. log1p_with_ln arm - t < -1/2 is outside the artanh validity wall, so these rows are evidence about ln's reduction, not the series
-0. -2834.
// generated by: flint, validated by: [mpmath, decimal] ; exact point and policy arm anchors: log1p(0) = 0 is the only exact value on the domain, and t = 1 / t = -1/2 ARE the log1p_artanh validity wall itself (src/policy/log1p.rs classify routes raw <= 10^SCALE and raw >= -(10^SCALE >> 1) to the artanh series) - the last inputs still series-routed, at every width and scale
0 0
// generated by: flint, validated by: [mpmath, decimal] ; near-zero one-ULP step: t = +/-10^-S at each tier's deepest scale - the smallest nonzero argument the cell can hold, where log1p(t) differs from t only at depth 2S and ln(1+t) would lose the whole result to cancellation. The nearest modes must return t exactly while the directed modes must step one ULP, so it separates the rounding modes on the tightest possible input. artanh arm
